From: claude <claude@p7x.dev>
Subject: [PATCH v2 1/4] libexec/mail: Rebuild a posted series from the list
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Change-Id: ywmmqzymxpnpukxuymoxyoyltmztztwy
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 03:34:43 +0000

A revised series needs an interdiff against its previous version, and
that version is what reached the list, not whatever its author still
has in their tree. The commits behind it may have been rewritten,
abandoned, or never existed locally, as with someone else's series.
series-rebuild.sh applies the posted patches and prints the range they
make, so the interdiff is computed from the mail the reviewer read.

It can apply them onto a commit other than their own base. A revision
is often rebased as well as changed, and an interdiff across two bases
buries the changes under everything that landed between them. Applied
onto the new base, the previous version differs from the new one only
by what the revision changed.

Each commit is dated from its message, so the same series always
rebuilds to the same commit ids.
